  <h1>When Should You Use CNC Machining Instead of 3D Printing?</h1>

  <p>When a part can be produced through either CNC machining or 3D printing, choosing between the two is not simply a matter of deciding which technology is newer, faster, or cheaper. Both processes can turn a digital design into a functional component, but they approach manufacturing in fundamentally different ways.</p>

  <p>CNC machining removes material from a solid piece of stock until the required geometry is produced. 3D printing creates the part layer by layer from a digital model. That difference affects what each process can manufacture efficiently, which materials can be used, how precisely features can be produced, what the finished surface looks like, and how much effort is required to make the final part.</p>

  <p>For some components, CNC machining is the more practical choice because the part depends on tight functional interfaces, production-grade material properties, predictable dimensions, or a controlled machined finish. For other components, 3D printing can make more sense because the geometry is highly complex, the design is still changing, the quantity is very small, or avoiding conventional tooling is more important.</p>

  <p>The real question is therefore not whether CNC machining is better than 3D printing. It is whether CNC machining is better suited to the specific part, material, geometry, production requirements, and stage of the product.</p>

  <h2>CNC Machining and 3D Printing Solve Different Manufacturing Problems</h2>

  <p>The most useful starting point is understanding how the two processes create a part.</p>

  <p>CNC machining is a subtractive process. The manufacturer starts with a block, bar, plate, or another form of stock material and removes material through cutting operations. The finished component is therefore produced from the physical properties of the selected stock.</p>

  <p>3D printing is an additive process. Instead of removing material from a larger piece, the machine builds the component layer by layer using a process-specific material such as filament, resin, powder, or metal feedstock.</p>

  <p>This fundamental difference creates two different manufacturing strengths.</p>

  <p>CNC machining is naturally suited to parts where precision, material performance, accessible geometry, and controlled surfaces are important. 3D printing is naturally suited to parts where geometric freedom, customization, rapid iteration, and the avoidance of conventional tooling provide greater value.</p>

  <p>Neither process should be selected in isolation from the part itself.</p>

  <h2>When CNC Machining Is the Better Choice</h2>

  <h3>When Dimensional Accuracy Is Critical</h3>

  <p>One of the strongest reasons to use CNC machining is when the part contains dimensions that directly control fit, alignment, movement, or assembly.</p>

  <p>A bearing seat, precision bore, mounting interface, threaded connection, sealing surface, or mating component may depend on carefully controlled dimensions. In those situations, the manufacturing process needs to provide predictable dimensional control across the features that matter.</p>

  <p>CNC machining is particularly well suited to this type of requirement because the cutting process directly produces the final feature from a solid workpiece. The machine can establish controlled dimensions and then verify those dimensions through inspection.</p>

  <p>That does not mean every CNC-machined part automatically has superior accuracy. Machine capability, tooling, material, workholding, programming, thermal conditions, and inspection all affect the result. The important point is that CNC machining provides a strong manufacturing route when the design places a high value on precise machined features.</p>

  <h3>When the Part Needs Production-Grade Material Properties</h3>

  <p>A prototype may only need to demonstrate the shape of a product. A functional component may need to behave like the final production part.</p>

  <p>This distinction matters when material performance is part of the reason the component exists.</p>

  <p>CNC machining can produce parts directly from a wide range of engineering metals and plastics in stock form. That can be valuable when the part needs the actual material characteristics required for service rather than a material selected mainly because it is easy to print.</p>

  <p>A structural bracket, mechanical housing, precision shaft, fixture, or industrial mounting component may depend on the strength, stiffness, wear resistance, thermal behavior, or corrosion resistance of a specific production material.</p>

  <p>When those characteristics are critical, CNC machining may provide a more direct path from the engineering specification to the final material.</p>

  <h3>When Mechanical Performance Matters</h3>

  <p>Manufacturing method can influence how a material behaves in the finished component.</p>

  <p>In CNC machining, the part is cut from a solid workpiece. In many 3D-printing technologies, material is deposited or fused layer by layer, which can produce process-dependent directional behavior.</p>

  <p>The exact behavior depends on the printing technology and material, so it would be inaccurate to treat every printed part as mechanically weak. Industrial additive manufacturing can produce highly capable functional components.</p>

  <p>The more useful distinction is whether the application can tolerate process-specific material behavior.</p>

  <p>If a component experiences repeated mechanical loading, needs predictable interfaces, or is expected to reproduce the characteristics of a conventional production material, CNC machining can become more attractive.</p>

  <h3>When Surface Finish Is Important</h3>

  <p>CNC machining can also be a strong choice when the required surface condition is naturally aligned with a machined finish.</p>

  <p>3D-printed surfaces can contain process-specific texture, layer effects, stair-stepping, or other characteristics that may require post-processing before the component reaches its intended finish.</p>

  <p>CNC machining can produce controlled machined surfaces directly and can also support additional finishing processes where the application requires them.</p>

  <p>This becomes particularly important for visible components, sealing surfaces, sliding interfaces, bearing locations, and other areas where surface condition affects either appearance or function.</p>

  <h3>When the Geometry Is Relatively Accessible</h3>

  <p>CNC machining works particularly well when cutting tools can reach the required features without excessive complexity.</p>

  <p>Accessible pockets, holes, contours, bosses, mounting faces, and external surfaces can often be produced efficiently through conventional machining.</p>

  <p>The more a design depends on hidden surfaces, deep internal cavities, extreme undercuts, or geometry that cannot be reached easily by the available tools, the more the machining strategy can become complicated.</p>

  <p>This does not mean complex geometry automatically excludes CNC machining. Multi-axis machining, special tooling, multiple setups, and processes such as EDM can extend machining capability significantly.</p>

  <p>The more useful question is whether the geometry can be produced without creating disproportionate manufacturing effort.</p>

  <h3>When the Design Is Stable</h3>

  <p>Design stability can also influence the decision.</p>

  <p>When a design is changing every few days, the ability to update the physical part directly from a revised digital model can make 3D printing extremely useful.</p>

  <p>Once the design becomes stable, the reasons for choosing CNC may become stronger, particularly when the final component requires precision, production-grade materials, or better surface characteristics.</p>

  <p>This is why the same product may use 3D printing during early development and CNC machining later.</p>

  <h2>When 3D Printing Is the Better Choice</h2>

  <h3>When Geometry Is the Main Challenge</h3>

  <p>Geometry is one of the clearest areas where 3D printing can outperform conventional machining.</p>

  <p>CNC machining is constrained by the physical reach of cutting tools. The tool needs access to the material being removed, and that creates limitations around certain internal structures, undercuts, narrow cavities, deep features, and hidden surfaces.</p>

  <p>Additive manufacturing changes that relationship.</p>

  <p>Because the part is built rather than carved from a solid block, certain complex shapes can be created without requiring a cutting tool to physically reach every internal surface.</p>

  <p>This is particularly relevant for lattice structures, internal channels, lightweighting features, organic geometries, and other forms that can be difficult to create economically through subtractive machining.</p>

  <p>For these applications, the geometric freedom of additive manufacturing can be more valuable than the precision advantages of CNC machining.</p>

  <h3>When Rapid Design Iteration Matters</h3>

  <p>During product development, the design may change frequently.</p>

  <p>A mounting hole moves.</p>

  <p>A wall becomes thicker.</p>

  <p>An internal passage is redesigned.</p>

  <p>A housing receives a different connector.</p>

  <p>These changes can be inexpensive to implement in a digital model, but the manufacturing process still determines how quickly the revised design can become a physical part.</p>

  <p>3D printing is particularly attractive in this stage because another build can often be started from the updated geometry without creating a new conventional mold or changing dedicated tooling.</p>

  <p>This makes additive manufacturing valuable not only because it can produce the part, but because it can shorten the feedback loop between design change and physical validation.</p>

  <h3>When Avoiding Tooling Matters</h3>

  <p>Traditional tooling can create a significant upfront commitment.</p>

  <p>For a small quantity or a rapidly changing design, that commitment may not make sense.</p>

  <p>3D printing can remove much of this conventional tooling requirement and allow production to begin directly from the digital design.</p>

  <p>This can be particularly useful for prototypes, customized products, small production runs, replacement components, and early-stage products where future demand remains uncertain.</p>

  <p>The absence of tooling does not mean 3D printing is always cheaper. Large or complex printed parts can still require substantial material and machine time. The advantage is that the process changes the nature of the upfront commitment.</p>

  <h3>When Only a Few Parts Are Needed</h3>

  <p>Very small production quantities can change the economics of both processes.</p>

  <p>CNC machining can involve programming, stock preparation, fixturing, and setup. Those costs can be significant when only one or two parts are required.</p>

  <p>3D printing can often move from digital geometry to a physical part without the same type of dedicated setup or tooling.</p>

  <p>That can make additive manufacturing attractive for very small runs, especially when the geometry is complex and the functional requirements do not demand machining-level surfaces or dimensional control.</p>

  <p>But quantity should never be treated as an automatic answer.</p>

  <p>A simple aluminum part may be straightforward and economical to machine even when only a small number of pieces are required.</p>

  <h3>When Customization Is Important</h3>

  <p>Products that require many individual variations can be difficult to manufacture economically with tooling-based processes.</p>

  <p>3D printing is naturally suited to this situation because the digital design can change from one build to another without requiring an entirely new physical mold for every variation.</p>

  <p>This makes additive manufacturing valuable for personalized components, low-volume variants, specialized equipment, replacement parts, and products with frequently changing configurations.</p>

  <h2>Geometry Can Change the Entire Decision</h2>

  <p>The most important part of a CNC-versus-3D-printing decision may be the geometry itself.</p>

  <p>Imagine a relatively simple aluminum bracket with accessible holes, flat mounting faces, and a few pockets. The geometry does not place unusual demands on the cutting tools. If the part also requires tight interfaces and production-grade aluminum, CNC machining can provide a direct manufacturing route.</p>

  <p>Now imagine a lightweight bracket containing internal channels, complex lattice structures, organic surfaces, and several enclosed features. The same basic function may now require a much more complicated machining strategy.</p>

  <p>3D printing may offer a better way to create that geometry because the manufacturing process does not depend on the same tool-access limitations.</p>

  <p>The difference is not that one technology is capable and the other is incapable.</p>

  <p>The difference is how much manufacturing effort is required to achieve the same geometry.</p>

  <h2>Material Can Change the Decision</h2>

  <p>Material should be evaluated before deciding between CNC machining and 3D printing.</p>

  <p>CNC machining offers access to a broad range of conventional engineering materials, including aluminum, stainless steel, steel, brass, copper, titanium, and engineering plastics.</p>

  <p>3D printing provides a different material ecosystem that depends heavily on the printing technology. Industrial additive processes can work with polymers, resins, nylon-based materials, metals, and other specialized feedstocks.</p>

  <p>The important question is not how many materials each technology supports.</p>

  <p>The question is:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>Which process can produce the required material characteristics in the finished component?</p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>A prototype may tolerate a printed polymer, while the final component requires machined aluminum or another engineering material.</p>

  <p>Conversely, a complex polymer geometry may be easier to produce through additive manufacturing than by machining it from plastic stock.</p>

  <p>Material selection and process selection should therefore be considered together.</p>

  <h2>Which Process Gives Better Dimensional Control?</h2>

  <p>When dimensional control is one of the primary requirements, CNC machining generally deserves serious consideration.</p>

  <p>The process can directly create precision features through controlled cutting operations, and critical dimensions can be inspected after machining.</p>

  <p>3D printing technologies can also produce accurate components, particularly industrial systems designed for engineering production, but their dimensional behavior depends on the technology, material, geometry, build orientation, thermal behavior, and post-processing.</p>

  <p>Some printed parts may require machining after printing when specific features need tighter dimensional control.</p>

  <p>This creates an important hybrid possibility:</p>

  <p><strong>3D printing for complexity, CNC machining for precision.</strong></p>

  <p>When both characteristics matter, the technologies do not necessarily have to compete.</p>

  <h2>Which Process Produces Stronger Functional Parts?</h2>

  <p>Mechanical performance depends on much more than manufacturing method.</p>

  <p>Material choice, geometry, loading conditions, wall thickness, temperature, environment, build orientation, heat treatment, and finishing can all influence the result.</p>

  <p>However, the manufacturing process can still matter.</p>

  <p>CNC machining starts from a solid material whose properties are established before the part is cut. In many additive processes, the final material structure is influenced by how layers are deposited or fused.</p>

  <p>For applications where predictable bulk material behavior and strong machined interfaces are important, CNC machining can provide a straightforward route.</p>

  <p>For applications where geometric optimization is more important than conventional material behavior, additive manufacturing may provide advantages that cannot be achieved economically through machining.</p>

  <h2>Surface Finish and Post-Processing Can Decide the Outcome</h2>

  <p>The primary manufacturing process is only part of the final production route.</p>

  <p>A printed component may require support removal, sanding, machining, polishing, coating, or another finishing operation.</p>

  <p>A CNC-machined component may require deburring, anodizing, plating, painting, polishing, heat treatment, or other finishing depending on the application.</p>

  <p>This means the correct comparison is not simply:</p>

  <p><strong>CNC versus 3D printing.</strong></p>

  <p>The better comparison is:</p>

  <p><strong>CNC process + required finishing versus 3D-printing process + required finishing.</strong></p>

  <p>A process that appears inexpensive at the machine stage can become less attractive after all required post-processing is included.</p>

  <h2>When Precision Is the Main Requirement</h2>

  <p>There are situations where precision dominates the decision.</p>

  <p>Consider a component with a bearing interface, several critical bores, a sealing surface, controlled mounting locations, and a requirement for a smooth machined finish.</p>

  <p>The part may not benefit significantly from additive geometry freedom because its main engineering challenge is not creating an unusual shape.</p>

  <p>Its challenge is producing important interfaces consistently.</p>

  <p>In this situation, CNC machining can provide a direct path from engineering drawing to functional component.</p>

  <p>When a project depends heavily on those relationships, <a href="https://www.getzshape.com/">precision machining expertise</a> can be especially valuable when determining whether CNC is the more appropriate process for the component.</p>

  <p>The key is not simply machine availability. The manufacturer also needs to understand workholding, tooling, tolerances, inspection, material behavior, and the relationship between the critical features.</p>

  <h2>When Complex Geometry Is the Main Requirement</h2>

  <p>Now consider a component designed to reduce weight through internal structures and complex channels.</p>

  <p>The part might contain geometry that is technically possible through advanced multi-axis machining, but producing it could require several orientations, specialized tools, complicated fixturing, extensive material removal, or multiple manufacturing operations.</p>

  <p>3D printing may be more attractive because the geometry itself becomes easier to produce.</p>

  <p>The advantage is especially relevant when the complex geometry has a functional purpose rather than existing only for appearance.</p>

  <p>Internal cooling channels, lightweight lattice structures, integrated ducts, organic topology-optimized forms, and certain consolidated assemblies are examples where additive manufacturing can provide a design advantage that conventional machining struggles to reproduce economically.</p>

  <h2>Production Quantity Changes the Economics</h2>

  <p>Production quantity should be considered, but it should not be used as a rigid decision rule.</p>

  <p>A very small quantity can make tooling-heavy processes difficult to justify, especially when the design is still changing.</p>

  <p>CNC machining and 3D printing can both support low-volume production, but their economics depend on the individual part.</p>

  <p>CNC cost can increase with machining time, material removal, setups, and fixturing.</p>

  <p>3D-printing cost can increase with build volume, material consumption, build time, orientation, support requirements, and post-processing.</p>

  <p>As quantity changes, those factors scale differently.</p>

  <p>That is why an engineer should compare the complete cost of producing the required quantity instead of assuming that one technology is always cheaper below a specific number of parts.</p>

  <h2>Lead Time Is More Than Machine Time</h2>

  <p>It is easy to assume that 3D printing must always be faster because it can go directly from a digital model to a physical part.</p>

  <p>The reality is more nuanced.</p>

  <p>A printed part may require preparation, build time, cooling, support removal, finishing, and inspection.</p>

  <p>A CNC part may require programming, stock preparation, fixturing, machining, deburring, finishing, and inspection.</p>

  <p>A simple machined component can therefore be produced very quickly, while a complex printed part can take considerably longer to build and finish.</p>

  <p>The meaningful comparison is the time from an approved design to a usable finished component.</p>

  <h2>What Happens When the Design Is Still Changing?</h2>

  <p>Design maturity can be one of the strongest reasons to choose 3D printing over CNC.</p>

  <p>When engineers are still making frequent geometric changes, additive manufacturing allows those changes to flow quickly from the CAD model into another physical version.</p>

  <p>This is especially useful when the purpose of the prototype is to learn.</p>

  <p>The team may be testing assembly, ergonomics, fit, airflow, weight distribution, or another design characteristic and needs several physical versions before the geometry becomes stable.</p>

  <p>CNC machining can still be used during development, particularly when functional testing requires real production materials or precise interfaces.</p>

  <p>The decision therefore depends on what the prototype needs to prove.</p>

  <h2>Prototype Material and Final Production Material Are Not Always the Same</h2>

  <p>A visual prototype can sometimes be manufactured from a material that behaves very differently from the eventual production component.</p>

  <p>That may be acceptable when the goal is simply to confirm shape or assembly.</p>

  <p>It becomes less acceptable when the prototype is being used to evaluate mechanical performance, thermal behavior, wear, sealing, structural stiffness, or another property that depends strongly on the final material.</p>

  <p>CNC machining can be especially useful in this situation because the prototype can be manufactured from the actual production-grade material.</p>

  <p>This can make the prototype a closer representation of the eventual product.</p>

  <h2>When CNC and 3D Printing Should Be Combined</h2>

  <p>One of the most useful outcomes of a CNC-versus-3D-printing comparison is realizing that the answer does not always have to be one or the other.</p>

  <p>A component can be 3D printed to create a complex overall geometry and then CNC machined where precise interfaces are required.</p>

  <p>This can be particularly useful for metal additive manufacturing, where the additive process creates geometry that would be difficult to machine while CNC machining establishes the critical dimensions that require tighter control.</p>

  <p>The same principle can apply to polymer parts.</p>

  <p>A printed component can receive machining on a mounting surface, bore, threaded feature, or another critical interface.</p>

  <p>This approach allows each process to perform the part of the manufacturing work it handles most effectively.</p>

  <p>Instead of forcing one technology to produce every characteristic of the part, the manufacturing strategy can be divided according to function.</p>

  <h2>Do Not Choose CNC Simply Because the Part Is Metal</h2>

  <p>Metal does not automatically mean CNC machining is the correct choice.</p>

  <p>Industrial metal additive manufacturing can produce components with highly complex geometry that may be impractical to machine conventionally.</p>

  <p>The important question is whether the geometry, material, production quantity, accuracy, surface requirements, and economics support additive manufacturing.</p>

  <p>Conversely, do not choose metal 3D printing simply because the geometry looks advanced.</p>

  <p>If the component is relatively simple and requires tight interfaces, machining may be a more direct and economical solution.</p>

  <p>Geometry and requirements should drive the decision, not the perceived sophistication of the technology.</p>

  <h2>Do Not Choose 3D Printing Simply Because the Quantity Is Small</h2>

  <p>Low volume is one reason to investigate additive manufacturing, but it is not enough by itself.</p>

  <p>A small aluminum bracket with straightforward geometry, accessible surfaces, and precision mounting holes may be easier to CNC machine than to print.</p>

  <p>A small complex housing with internal structures may be the opposite.</p>

  <p>Both are low-volume parts.</p>

  <p>Their manufacturing requirements are different.</p>

  <p>Quantity should therefore be combined with geometry, material, precision, surface finish, and production requirements before the process is selected.</p>

  <h2>How Design for Manufacturing Changes the Decision</h2>

  <p>Design for manufacturability can reveal why one process is more appropriate than another.</p>

  <p>A machined part may become significantly easier to produce when internal corners use practical radii, deep pockets are reduced where function allows, walls remain sufficiently rigid, and important features are accessible from a reasonable number of orientations.</p>

  <p>An additive part may become more efficient when the build orientation is considered early, support requirements are minimized, wall thickness is appropriate, and critical surfaces are planned for post-processing.</p>

  <p>DFM is therefore not a rule that automatically favors CNC or 3D printing.</p>

  <p>It is a way of understanding how the design interacts with the manufacturing process.</p>

  <h2>A Practical Example: Precision Aluminum Housing</h2>

  <p>Consider a small aluminum housing used in industrial equipment.</p>

  <p>The housing includes several mounting holes, precision bores, machined mating surfaces, internal pockets, and an anodized exterior.</p>

  <p>The part is required in a relatively small batch.</p>

  <p>CNC machining may be a strong candidate because the material is available as stock, the critical features require dimensional control, and the overall geometry is accessible to cutting tools.</p>

  <p>3D printing could still be considered if the housing contained highly complex internal channels that would otherwise require extensive machining.</p>

  <p>If additive manufacturing were selected, the critical interfaces could potentially receive CNC finishing afterward.</p>

  <p>The decision therefore depends on whether geometric freedom or precision and production material characteristics are the dominant requirement.</p>

  <h2>A Practical Example: Complex Lightweight Component</h2>

  <p>Now consider a lightweight metal component designed with internal lattice structures, curved surfaces, and integrated channels.</p>

  <p>Machining the part from a solid block could create substantial material waste and require difficult tool access.</p>

  <p>Advanced metal 3D printing may offer a way to create the overall geometry with far less dependence on conventional cutting access.</p>

  <p>However, the component may still need CNC machining on critical mounting surfaces or holes.</p>

  <p>In this case, additive manufacturing can create the complexity while machining provides the precision where it matters.</p>

  <h2>A Practical Example: Simple Plastic Prototype</h2>

  <p>Consider a plastic enclosure that is still undergoing frequent design changes.</p>

  <p>The purpose of the first prototypes is to validate fit, connector placement, external dimensions, and assembly.</p>

  <p>If only a few versions are required, 3D printing can provide a rapid way to produce each revision without conventional tooling.</p>

  <p>CNC machining may become more relevant later if the product requires a specific production-grade plastic, precise interfaces, or a better final surface condition.</p>

  <p>Again, the manufacturing process can change as the product moves from learning to production.</p>

  <h2>How to Compare CNC and 3D Printing Before You Decide</h2>

  <p>Start with the function of the part.</p>

  <p>Ask what the component needs to withstand, support, contain, align, connect, or accomplish.</p>

  <p>Then examine the material.</p>

  <p>Determine whether the final part needs a specific metal, engineering plastic, additive material, or another material system.</p>

  <p>Next, study the geometry.</p>

  <p>Look at tool access, internal cavities, undercuts, wall thickness, feature accessibility, and the amount of material that would need to be removed or deposited.</p>

  <p>After that, identify the critical dimensions and surfaces.</p>

  <p>Determine whether the part depends on tight fits, controlled bores, sealing surfaces, threads, flatness, or another precision requirement.</p>

  <p>Then consider production quantity and design stability.</p>

  <p>A stable design with repeat demand creates a different manufacturing decision from a constantly changing prototype.</p>

  <p>Finally, compare the complete manufacturing route.</p>

  <p>Include setup, build or machining time, material, tooling, finishing, inspection, and any secondary operations required to deliver the finished component.</p>

  <h2>What Should You Choose for Your Part?</h2>

  <p>CNC machining is generally worth considering first when the part depends heavily on precision, production-grade material properties, controlled machined interfaces, or a predictable finished surface.</p>

  <p>3D printing deserves stronger consideration when geometric freedom, rapid design iteration, customization, low tooling commitment, or difficult internal structures are central to the project.</p>

  <p>There are also situations where both processes belong in the same manufacturing route.</p>

  <p>The best process is the one that creates the required part with an appropriate balance of technical performance, manufacturing effort, cost, lead time, and production flexibility.</p>

  <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

  <p>CNC machining and 3D printing should not be viewed as direct substitutes in every situation.</p>

  <p>CNC machining is fundamentally strong when the manufacturing challenge is precision, material performance, accessible geometry, repeatable dimensions, and controlled surfaces.</p>

  <p>3D printing is fundamentally strong when the manufacturing challenge is geometric freedom, rapid iteration, customization, or the avoidance of conventional tooling.</p>

  <p>The decision becomes much easier when the question is framed around the part rather than the technology.</p>

  <p>What does the part need to do?</p>

  <p>What material does it need?</p>

  <p>How complex is the geometry?</p>

  <p>Which dimensions actually matter?</p>

  <p>How many parts are required?</p>

  <p>How stable is the design?</p>

  <p>What finish is required?</p>

  <p>What post-processing will be necessary?</p>

  <p>What is the complete cost and lead time?</p>

  <p>Once those questions are answered, the process often becomes much clearer.</p>

  <p>Use CNC machining when the part's value depends on precision, predictable material properties, and controlled production characteristics.</p>

  <p>Use 3D printing when the part's value depends on geometric freedom, flexibility, rapid iteration, or avoiding conventional tooling.</p>

  <p>And when the part requires both characteristics, there is no reason to force a choice between them. A hybrid manufacturing route can use additive manufacturing for complexity and CNC machining for the features where precision matters most.</p>
